course: arts
lesson: 29
title: Dance: from TikTok dances to ballet
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Dance is moving to music on purpose, and it comes in every size: a fifteen-second phone dance, a breaking battle, an Irish jig, a Ukrainian hopak or a three-hour ballet.
explain: Every generation has its craze dance: your gran may have done the twist in the 1960s, your parents the Macarena in the 1990s, and you learn a new one online in ten minutes.
explain: Behind every smooth dance video are dozens of falls and failed tries you never see, because people post only their best take.
explain: The hardest dances, like ballet on the tips of the toes or the big squat-kicks of hopak, need years of training for strength and balance, so dancers build up slowly with a teacher.
explain: People say it their own way: in the north of England "nowt" means nothing, in Ireland "gom" means a fool, "well hard" in Britain means very difficult, and "facts" in American slang means "that's true".
fact: Short viral dances spread fast online, and every generation has had craze dances, like the twist in the 1960s or the Macarena in the 1990s.
fact: Ballet dancers dance en pointe, on the tips of their toes, in special stiff shoes after years of training; teachers wait until feet are strong enough, often around age 11 or 12.
fact: Ballet has many famous male dancers, such as Rudolf Nureyev and Mikhail Baryshnikov, and it needs huge strength and athletic skill.
fact: Falling is part of learning to dance, and many viral videos took the dancer dozens of tries.
fact: Breaking, or breakdancing, started in New York in the 1970s and was an Olympic sport for the first time at Paris 2024.
fact: Breaking was not chosen for the Los Angeles 2028 Olympics.
fact: In Irish stepdance, competition dancers keep their arms still and move their feet very fast; the show Riverdance made it world famous in 1994.
fact: A popular story says Irish dancers kept their arms still to hide dancing from rulers, but historians say there is no evidence for it.
fact: Almost everyone can learn to dance with practice and by listening for the beat, the steady pulse of the music.
fact: Hopak is a lively Ukrainian folk dance with big jumps and squat-kicks, linked to Cossack traditions.
fact: Viral dances are made by real people, often young creators, and naming them when you copy their dance is fair.
fact: Dancing in a group first can build the confidence to dance alone, and many dancers are shy off stage.
exercise: Chloe's gran says she did the twist all night in 1962. What does this show? | Every generation has its craze dances | Dance crazes were invented by TikTok and did not exist before the internet | People did not really dance at all in the years before mobile phones | Crazes last for ever
exercise: How do ballet dancers stand on the tips of their toes? | Special stiff shoes and years of training | Their toes are naturally made of bone only | They hang on hidden wires | Any beginner can do it on the first day
exercise: Why do ballet teachers wait before beginners go on their toes? | Feet must grow strong first | It is just a rule to make children wait, so they want it more | Pointe shoes are only ever sold to grown-ups in special shops | Toes stop growing at age five, so it is too late after that anyway
exercise: Zach says ballet is "just for girls". Which is true? | Many great ballet dancers are men | Men have never danced ballet anywhere in the world, only women | Ballet is far too easy for boys, so they choose harder sports | Boys are too heavy
exercise: Dani lands on her bum copying a TikTok move. What is true? | Falling is part of learning | She is just bad at dancing and will be for the rest of her life | Real dancers always get every move right on the very first try | It is impossible
exercise: Where did breaking start? | In New York in the 1970s | In Paris in 2024 | In ancient Greece | In Ireland in 1994
exercise: When was breaking first an Olympic sport? | At Paris 2024 | At London 2012 | At Los Angeles 2028 | It has never been in the Olympics
exercise: Molly keeps her arms stiff in Irish dancing. Why? | Irish stepdance keeps the arms still | She is being punished by her dance teacher for talking in class | Her arms are far too tired from practising to move any more | Her arms are broken
exercise: Is the story that Irish dancers hid their arms from rulers true? | No evidence, say historians | Yes, every single history book about Ireland proves that it is true | Yes, there was a law in Ireland that banned dancers from moving their arms | It was a TV story
exercise: Ben says he has "two left feet". What is true? | Almost anyone can learn | Some people can never dance at all, however much they practise | Dancing is only possible for people who are born with good rhythm | Having two left feet is a real medical problem that doctors treat
exercise: What is the beat? | The steady pulse of the music | The loudest instrument in the song | A dance move with the feet | The end of a song
exercise: Olena's friend asks how her legs do not snap in hopak. What is the answer? | Hard training | Hopak is actually easy and needs no training at all to dance it | Ukrainian legs are made differently from everybody else's legs | Hopak dancers wear hidden metal leg braces under their wide trousers
exercise: What is hopak? | A lively Ukrainian folk dance with jumps and squat-kicks | A slow Japanese circle dance | A dance done on the tips of the toes | A breaking battle from New York
exercise: Kenji copies a dance and does not name the creator. What is fair? | Name the creator | Dances that are online belong to nobody, so anyone can take them | Only famous people need to be given credit for the dances they make | Copy the dance and tell everyone that he made it up himself
exercise: Tia is nervous to dance alone. What does her teacher do? | Starts with the whole class dancing | Makes her dance alone in front of everyone at once to get it over with | Tells her kindly that shy people are not able to dance well | She skips dancing
exercise: What made Irish dancing world famous in 1994? | The show Riverdance | The Olympic Games | A TikTok video | A ballet in Paris
test: Your mate goes, "Dance crazes are new, my nan never did one." What is likely true? | His nan had crazes too | Nobody really danced at all before the internet came along | Dancing was banned in most countries until the year 2000 | Only famous people on television danced in the past
test: What would happen if a beginner tried to dance on her toes with no training or shoes? | She could hurt her feet | She would float across the floor just like a professional dancer | Nothing would happen, because toes are already very strong | Her toes would grow stronger straight away and stop hurting
test: A kid from London says, "Ballet is for girls, bruv." What is the best reply? | Many top ballet dancers are men | He is right, boys are not able to do ballet properly at all | Ballet is for girls by law in Britain and most other countries | Ballet is too easy for boys
test: Hassan says "Breaking in the Olympics? Facts?" ("Facts?" means is that true?). Which is true? | Yes, at Paris 2024, though not chosen for LA 2028 | No, breaking was never in the Olympics | Yes, it has been in every Olympics since 1970 | Yes, and it will be in every Olympics for ever
test: In a cartoon, a boy learns a hard dance perfectly on his first go. What is more realistic? | It takes many tries | Every dancer gets every hard dance right on the very first try | Hard dances can be learned perfectly just by watching them once | Dance cannot be learned at all, you can either do it or you cannot
test: Molly's friend asks if she is "being punished" because her arms are still. What is the answer? | That is the style | Irish dancers are always punished for moving their arms in class | Her dance teacher tied her arms down so she would not wave them | She simply forgot to move her arms because she was so nervous
test: A tour guide says Irish dancers hid their arms "to fool the rulers". What is the honest reply? | It is a popular story, but historians find no evidence | It is a proven fact from old laws | The rulers made the dancers keep still | Irish dancers never moved at all
test: Your Gen Alpha cousin says Ben "has no rizz on the dance floor" ("rizz" means charm or cool). What would help Ben? | Simple steps and the beat | Giving up dancing for good and standing by the wall at every party | Copying the hardest and fastest dance he can find on the internet first | Dancing with no music
test: Kenji's dance went viral but the creator got no credit. What is the wisest move? | Add the creator's name to his video | Delete everything and never dance again | Say it does not matter who made it | Claim he made the dance himself
test: Your friend from Liverpool says hopak is "dead easy, just jumping, la" ("la" means mate). Which is true? | It needs hard training | It is the easiest dance in the whole world to learn | It needs no strength at all, only a bit of bouncing | Only small children are light enough to do it properly
test: Tia's friend says shy people "just can't dance". Which is true? | Many dancers are shy off stage | Shy people are not allowed to join dance classes in most schools | Shyness makes your feet stop working properly whenever music plays | Only loud people learn
exam: Identify the principal reason for which teachers delay the commencement of dancing en pointe. | The feet must first gain strength | The requisite shoes are available exclusively to adults by regulation | The practice is prohibited by law before the attainment of adulthood | The toes cease growing early
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the history of breaking at the Olympic Games? | It was first contested at Paris 2024 and was not selected for Los Angeles 2028 | It has been contested at every Olympic Games since 1970 | It has never been contested at any Olympic Games | It was first contested at London 2012
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the claim that Irish dancers concealed their arms from rulers. | Historians report no evidence supporting the claim | The claim is confirmed by contemporary legal records | The claim was established by the show Riverdance | The claim is required learning in Irish schools
exam: Ascertain the conduct most appropriate where a dance devised by another person is reproduced and published. | The naming of the creator of the dance | The assertion that one devised the dance oneself | The omission of any reference to its origin | Removal of the video
